<speak><prosody rate="90%"><mark name="hook"/><p>How do you prepare for a job you have never done? LearnSprint lets you practice a fictional first shift in operations, where your decisions change the plan.</p><break time="500ms"/><mark name="brief"/><p>Three orders share limited stock and delivery departures. I ask the warehouse what is available. The reply points to scenario facts, so I can distinguish confirmed stock from an expected arrival.</p><break time="500ms"/><mark name="plan"/><p>I allocate the kits and review the whole board. The application checks inventory over time, carrier capacity, order quantities, deadlines, and shipping cost. I decide when to record this plan.</p><break time="500ms"/><mark name="incident"/><p>Then the supplier is delayed. The earlier review stays in the record, but it no longer describes the current facts. I need to respond to the changed situation.</p><break time="500ms"/><mark name="agreement"/><p>Customer B can accept a split delivery. Asking reveals the offer; I must explicitly record the agreement before it changes the constraints. I move twenty kits to tomorrow and review the recovery plan.</p><break time="500ms"/><mark name="handoff"/><p>The handoff preserves my plan, its assumptions, and unresolved issues. This is evidence of decisions inside a simulation, not a claim that parcels were delivered or that I am ready for a job.</p><break time="500ms"/><mark name="replay"/><p>A linked replay changes the opening stock and customer terms. It starts with a clean board and keeps its own help history. The report compares the saved decisions, rather than treating a repeated answer as proof of learning.</p><break time="500ms"/><mark name="ai"/><p>Amazon Bedrock Nova Two Lite interprets a typed request into one bounded allocation proposal. I see the exact edit, choose Apply, and can Undo. The application still owns the facts and checks.</p><break time="500ms"/><mark name="close"/><p>This Alexa Plus style web simulation runs on AWS. Browser voice remains a prototype. Next comes practitioner review and a learner pilot. LearnSprint: practice the work before the first job.</p><break time="500ms"/></prosody></speak>